  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes ways to therapeutically target Eph receptors, contrasting the EphA2 and EphB branches across the cell membrane. It shows both extracellular and intracellular intervention points. Outside the cell, receptor signaling can be modulated by natural ligands such as ephrin-A and ephrin-B, as well as by antibodies, peptides, compounds, aptamers, and a soluble ephrin-B fusion protein. These approaches can either activate or block receptor interactions. An Eph-directed CAR T-cell strategy is also indicated as a cell-based therapy.\n\nAt the membrane and inside the cell, the diagram highlights receptor kinase activity, including ATP-dependent signaling, and shows several inhibitory strategies such as kinase inhibitors, allosteric kinase inhibitors, intracellular peptides, and targeted degraders. On the EphA2 side, a phosphorylation site labeled pS897 appears to be a notable regulatory point.\n\nThe bottom portion broadens the picture to gene-expression control, showing that Eph receptor levels may also be reduced at the RNA or gene level through miRNA, siRNA, and HDAC inhibition. Overall, the image presents Eph receptors as druggable at multiple layers—ligand binding, receptor signaling, protein stability, immune targeting, and transcriptional or post-transcriptional regulation.",
